 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T16:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**If you mean Kuala Lumpur, I wouldn’t keep otherwise well children home solely because of today’s reading—but I would keep them out of outdoor activities.** It’s Saturday, **October 17, 2026**, and at **4:00 p.m. KL time** the Cheras station reported a Malaysian Air Pollutant Index (API) of **164, “unhealthy.”** Check the reading near their school, since conditions can differ across KL. ([semakapi.my](https://semakapi.my/en/kuala-lumpur))\n\nIf they have a **Saturday class**, ask the school to confirm that recess, sports and other activities will be indoors. Malaysia’s education guidance calls for outdoor school activities to stop above API 100; the ministry said on October 15 that schools close when API reaches 200. **If either child has asthma, breathing symptoms, or is particularly affected by the haze, keeping them home is reasonable—notify the school.** ([web26.bernama.com](https://web26.bernama.com/en/general/news.php?id=2620854))\n\nFor the **next regular school day**, check the official APIMS reading and your school’s notice again rather than relying on today’s number. If a child’s breathing is worsening, seek medical advice promptly. ([eqms.doe.gov.my](https://eqms.doe.gov.my/apims/main?utm_source=openai))",
